course: dictionary
lesson: 6587
title: One word, several meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Many words have more than one meaning, and «nit» is one of them. Context tells you which meaning the writer means, so look at the words around it first.
explain: «Head lice (people often call them nits) spread through shared combs, brushes and hats.» Here a nit is the tiny egg of a head louse. The clue is head lice in hair, so it is not a silly person.
explain: In playground talk, «you nit!» is a mild, friendly insult. Always ask what meaning would fit, then try it in the sentence to see if it still makes sense.
fact: Nit has three main meanings: the egg or young of a head louse, a silly person (informal), and to pick nits, which means to find small, unimportant faults.
fact: A head louse is a tiny insect that lives in human hair.
fact: Head lice spread by head-to-head contact, and they cannot fly or jump.
fact: Head lice are common, and they are not a sign of being dirty.
fact: A nit comb is a fine comb used to find nits in hair.
exercise: «Head lice spread by head-to-head contact, and they lay eggs called nits on the hairs.» In this sentence, what is a nit? | A tiny insect that flies | *The egg of a head louse | A silly, foolish person | A small hairclip
exercise: Sam says, «Stop being such a nit, Ella!» What does nit mean here? | A comb for hair | An egg stuck to hair | To find small faults in someone's work | *A silly, foolish person
exercise: «Mrs Lee always nit-picks our spelling, circling every tiny mistake.» What does nit-picking mean? | *Finding small, unimportant faults | Ignoring the spelling completely | Writing very slowly | Praising every good word
test: «Shared helmets and hats can spread head lice and germs from one person to another.» What are nits? | Combs used for brushing | *Eggs of head lice, which stick to the hair | Hats that spread germs | Silly people
test: Which phrase means to find small, unimportant faults? | nit comb | have nits | *nit-picking | nitwit
exam: Which sentence uses nit to mean a silly person? | «Their eggs are called nits.» | *«You nit, you left your coat on the bus again!» | «Parents comb the hair with a nit comb.» | «Clean shared helmets and gloves, because they can spread head lice.»
